# A7 6.T1  Angle Pairs: Supplementary, Complementary, Vertical, Adjacent — Thread B, day 1 (MA.8.GR.1.4; MN 16.1, taught
# after the Unit 6 test so the angle facts are in play for the rest of the year; not on the Unit 6 test). Adjacent angles
# share a vertex and a side and do not overlap; vertical angles sit across a crossing and are equal; complementary
# angles make 90°, supplementary 180°, and a linear pair is supplementary. With expressions: name the pair, write its
# equation ("= 90", "= 180" or "equal"), solve, then put x back — the book names "reported x as the angle" three times.
# The state's named misconception for GR.1.4 — complementary and supplementary swapped — is board 4's option B. A
# 43-minute Wednesday: the deck is the usual 53 minutes by house rule, and nothing names a cut (HOUSE STYLE §9). An x on
# both sides is new to this class (A7 11.02 teaches it in full): Example 2 takes it off one side as its own line.
# Built from the benchmark and the B1G-M first (the unit outline), then read against Math Nation 16.1 (audit §9).
